--TEST--
`Core\Html::escape` writes all five of `&<"'` as character references and answers a `Core\Html\Markup` for a `tainted` argument — ADR 0024 §§ 3, 5, ADR 0133 §§ 1, 2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0024 § 3 writes this member out as *the* worked example of a launderer:
// narrow, named for one sink, and the only route from an untrusted value to
// text that may be written into an HTML document. The route runs end to end
// here — the value arrives tainted, the escape happens, and the answer is a
// `Core\Html\Markup`, which is the whole of what "it launders" means plus what
// ADR 0133 § 1 adds: the HTML sink escapes on its own and escaping is not
// idempotent, so the answer has to be a value the sink can tell from text.
tainted string $t = "<a href='x' title=\"y\">Tom & Jerry</a>";

// Declaring `string` here is the reject case now, and deliberately so — ADR
// 0133 § 2's "the eager-escape habit stops compiling". A contagious row's
// answer still carries the qualifier and cannot be bound to a plain type
// either (tests/conformance/core/a-launder-row-hands-a-tainted-value-to-the-sink-it-names.nvst).
Core\Html\Markup $safe = Core\Html::escape($t);
echo $safe, "\n";

// § 3's one way back to the bytes, which is what a question *about* the
// escaped text has to go through. The reason is written at the site and read
// by nobody, exactly as `Core\Secret::reveal`'s is.
string $source = Core\Html::toSource($safe, "asking which references the escape produced");

// All five, always, with no flag to choose between them — ADR 0063 R6 forbids
// the `htmlspecialchars` bitmask, and the member of every pair those flags
// chose between that is worth having is the safe one. Escaping both quote
// characters is what makes the answer safe in an unquoted attribute position
// as well as in element content, so this counts them rather than reading the
// line above: a member that dropped `'` back to PHP's pre-8.1 default still
// prints plausibly.
echo Core\Str::contains($source, "&amp;") ? "y" : "n",
     Core\Str::contains($source, "&lt;") ? "y" : "n",
     Core\Str::contains($source, "&gt;") ? "y" : "n",
     Core\Str::contains($source, "&quot;") ? "y" : "n",
     Core\Str::contains($source, "&#39;") ? "y" : "n",
     "\n";

// `&#39;` and not `&apos;`: the named reference is XML's, and HTML 4 never
// defined it.
echo Core\Str::contains($source, "&apos;") ? "y" : "n", "\n";

// The carrier and its source text are the same bytes and differ only in what
// the type system will let them reach — so `echo` of either writes the same
// line, and only one of them would survive a second trip through the sink.
echo $source == Core\Html::toSource($safe, "the round trip is the identity") ? "same" : "changed", "\n";
--EXPECT--
&lt;a href=&#39;x&#39; title=&quot;y&quot;&gt;Tom &amp; Jerry&lt;/a&gt;
yyyyy
n
same
